{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791295031,"nonce":"zBp28NC4O6iwfT2PZLMG6Pm6YiY=","title":"Rest arguments on static extensions are passed as scalar call arguments","message":"At e96908304fb79b891cfd790b3dcd5313beadd269, Issue10124 crashes in test1. Its `arr.add(5, 6, 7)` selects `Util.add\u003cT\u003e(arr:Array\u003cT\u003e, ...values:T)`, but final MIR calls a two-parameter function with four arguments. The callee treats scalar 5 as its rest array and faults at address 0xd.\n\nThe call typer packs rest arguments before resolving static extensions. The formal signature lookup stops at Array's built-in callbacks, so it never sees the extension's Rest\u003cT\u003e parameter. Extension formal types need receiver-derived generic bindings and must exclude the implicit receiver from the written argument positions.\n\nThe same row's test3 additionally requires a locally generic rest array with Int and Float arguments to infer Float and widen all numeric slots before array construction. This part is also recorded in 14e397f4f7017d07d9afb327903c8f7bc75c17383ab08be50b7fd31fb88ac619; its separate constructor inference scope remains open.\n\nBaseline: 854/1094 scored default tiered cases, 146 skipped. Full report /tmp/rayzor-generic-from-verified/report.tsv. Failing MIR /tmp/rayzor-rest10124-mir.log. Regression RestExtensionArguments.hx covers generic extension and direct static calls, empty and spread tails, Float tails including Int variables, local generic rest inference, and single evaluation of receiver and argument. Native Haxe passes before the compiler change.","files":null}]}